1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,865
It's Monday morning.

2
00:00:01,915 --> 00:00:05,074
There's a file
of almost three hundred and fifty lines,

3
00:00:05,074 --> 00:00:06,394
eighty-three notebooks,

4
00:00:06,394 --> 00:00:08,379
and results going back to February.

5
00:00:08,699 --> 00:00:10,350
Where does the next hour go?

6
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:13,472
Josh doesn't answer
that by planning everything first.

7
00:00:13,792 --> 00:00:16,935
He runs the code,
reads the first thing that breaks,

8
00:00:16,935 --> 00:00:18,414
and fixes only that.

9
00:00:18,464 --> 00:00:20,960
This episode turns that habit into a method.

10
00:00:23,233 --> 00:00:27,013
Start with what works.
With only the identity model,

11
00:00:27,013 --> 00:00:33,409
the whole pipeline already runs on toy data:
setup, train, predict, evaluate. No errors.

12
00:00:33,566 --> 00:00:38,942
That's a walking skeleton: every stage exists,
end to end, even though most are thin.

13
00:00:39,262 --> 00:00:43,486
From here, you thicken one bone at a time,
and the skeleton keeps walking.

14
00:00:44,066 --> 00:00:46,775
But look at what evaluate returned: nothing.

15
00:00:47,095 --> 00:00:49,058
The scores were computed and dropped.

16
00:00:49,378 --> 00:00:54,562
So the first task writes itself:
return the scores as a table, models by metrics.

17
00:00:56,900 --> 00:00:59,247
Now add scGen and keep running.

18
00:00:59,567 --> 00:01:02,276
Pause and predict: which stage breaks first?

19
00:01:06,433 --> 00:01:10,170
We ran each stage on toy data,
with a stand-in for scgen,

20
00:01:10,170 --> 00:01:13,580
patching only the smallest thing
needed to reach it.

21
00:01:13,900 --> 00:01:16,887
Here's the trail,
in the order the program reaches it.

22
00:01:17,033 --> 00:01:21,147
Setup: an attribute error,
x_dimensions, line 253.

23
00:01:21,147 --> 00:01:24,603
That was episode two. Train: a type error.

24
00:01:24,603 --> 00:01:30,708
Train is missing its training data,
because line 133 calls it with nothing.

25
00:01:30,867 --> 00:01:33,310
Predict: a name error at line 288,

26
00:01:33,310 --> 00:01:37,694
a helper that lives inside train,
where predict can't see it.

27
00:01:38,014 --> 00:01:42,297
Evaluate:
once mouse and human have different cell counts,

28
00:01:42,297 --> 00:01:46,653
as real data do,
R squared can't line them up, at line 313.

29
00:01:46,833 --> 00:01:50,865
Each error is one card on your to-do board.
Along scGen's path,

30
00:01:50,865 --> 00:01:55,601
the program hands you the order.
You only choose which path to walk first.

31
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:01,397
Some cards aren't errors yet.

32
00:02:01,397 --> 00:02:06,293
Josh's notebook shows a mouse training file
with about eighteen thousand genes,

33
00:02:06,293 --> 00:02:11,312
and a human one with almost sixty-two thousand.
The test files hold one thousand.

34
00:02:11,467 --> 00:02:15,222
One network can't read them all
until they share one gene axis.

35
00:02:15,542 --> 00:02:20,256
Lining up genes is a data job,
so it belongs next to the data classes.

36
00:02:20,433 --> 00:02:23,708
But for your first replication,
it's already done.

37
00:02:23,708 --> 00:02:28,031
The M1 version eight file
has one shared list of a thousand genes.

38
00:02:28,031 --> 00:02:33,010
It's one table plus a split recipe,
while your data classes take four files.

39
00:02:33,010 --> 00:02:34,337
So the real card is:

40
00:02:34,337 --> 00:02:38,119
split that table
into training and test, mouse and human.

41
00:02:41,200 --> 00:02:45,424
Pause: out of eighty-three notebooks,
which would you replicate first, and why?

42
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:50,958
Not a notebook.

43
00:02:51,008 --> 00:02:56,116
Pick the first cut of what your project
already calls its frozen benchmark:

44
00:02:56,116 --> 00:02:59,861
M1, version eight, holding out
non-classical monocytes.

45
00:03:00,181 --> 00:03:02,202
Its spec is m1_modern.yaml,

46
00:03:02,202 --> 00:03:08,864
and Josh already pasted its model settings
into the core file, a little above scGenModel.

47
00:03:09,033 --> 00:03:13,269
Its scorecard,
written by the notebook twenty-two v08 results,

48
00:03:13,269 --> 00:03:15,113
records the numbers to hit.

49
00:03:15,433 --> 00:03:19,422
The identity R squared of means
is zero point five five four.

50
00:03:19,742 --> 00:03:24,815
It's the same on every M1 version eight row,
because it describes the data:

51
00:03:24,815 --> 00:03:28,062
mouse test cells, unchanged, against human ones.

52
00:03:28,382 --> 00:03:31,006
No training and no sampling are involved,

53
00:03:31,006 --> 00:03:34,270
so once your package reads
the same file and split,

54
00:03:34,270 --> 00:03:38,814
and computes R squared of means,
it should reproduce it almost exactly.

55
00:03:39,134 --> 00:03:44,404
Comparing means,
not cells, also removes the crash at line 313.

56
00:03:44,567 --> 00:03:49,879
Then scGen: zero point nine two seven,
from the cellot autoencoder,

57
00:03:49,879 --> 00:03:54,636
whose run sits on the cluster
under cellot's results folder.

58
00:03:54,956 --> 00:03:57,388
To aim for it, your adapter wraps that model.

59
00:03:57,708 --> 00:04:01,402
It averages eighty-cell samples,
so compute it the same way,

60
00:04:01,402 --> 00:04:04,727
and since training adds noise,
aim within a tolerance.

61
00:04:05,047 --> 00:04:07,580
And you don't need to trace all of cellot:

62
00:04:07,580 --> 00:04:11,319
open only the few calls your adapter makes,
from episode four.

63
00:04:11,639 --> 00:04:13,439
Your own scorecard is the check.

64
00:04:18,867 --> 00:04:20,481
Where does SOLID fit?

65
00:04:20,531 --> 00:04:23,518
Treat it as a compass
for the moment something hurts,

66
00:04:23,518 --> 00:04:25,318
not a checklist for day one.

67
00:04:25,416 --> 00:04:27,955
Each letter already points
at a spot in this file.

68
00:04:28,134 --> 00:04:31,919
Single responsibility:
the data classes only load data,

69
00:04:31,919 --> 00:04:35,430
so gene alignment
and splitting belong beside them.

70
00:04:35,750 --> 00:04:41,254
Open-closed: adding CellOT should add a class,
not edit the chain of ifs in setup.

71
00:04:41,574 --> 00:04:44,176
A dictionary from names to classes does that.

72
00:04:44,334 --> 00:04:48,409
Liskov substitution:
every model must keep the contract's promise.

73
00:04:48,729 --> 00:04:52,803
The contract's train takes training data,
but IdentityModel's takes none.

74
00:04:53,123 --> 00:04:59,395
Line 133 followed IdentityModel,
so scGen, which keeps the contract, broke.

75
00:04:59,715 --> 00:05:03,811
Python checks names, not inputs,
so only a test can catch this.

76
00:05:03,967 --> 00:05:08,217
Interface segregation:
an evaluation needs one method,

77
00:05:08,217 --> 00:05:12,751
and its contract asks for only one.
Dependency inversion:

78
00:05:12,751 --> 00:05:15,694
the Experiment talks to the contract.

79
00:05:15,694 --> 00:05:21,204
It never imports the scgen library;
only scGenModel does, at line 248.

80
00:05:21,367 --> 00:05:25,669
And refactoring, meaning reshaping code
without changing what it does,

81
00:05:25,669 --> 00:05:28,066
can wait until the third model arrives.

82
00:05:32,533 --> 00:05:37,951
So here's the loop for every session.
Write the call you wish worked, as a small test.

83
00:05:37,951 --> 00:05:43,242
Run it. Read the first error it hits:
its message is the last line of the traceback.

84
00:05:43,400 --> 00:05:46,216
Make the smallest change
that gets past that line.

85
00:05:46,536 --> 00:05:49,586
When the test passes, commit,
and pick up the next card.

86
00:05:49,733 --> 00:05:51,515
Your cards, in order.

87
00:05:51,515 --> 00:05:56,519
Walk identity's path first,
because it has an exact answer:

88
00:05:56,519 --> 00:06:00,541
make evaluate return a table, split the M1 file,

89
00:06:00,541 --> 00:06:07,075
and compute R squared of means
until identity gives zero point five five four.

90
00:06:07,075 --> 00:06:15,034
Then walk scGen's trail, lines 253, 133 and 288,
until it lands near zero point nine two seven.

91
00:06:15,200 --> 00:06:19,386
Recap. The skeleton already walks,
the errors give the order,

92
00:06:19,386 --> 00:06:23,778
the first golden number
is identity's zero point five five four,

93
00:06:23,778 --> 00:06:28,512
and SOLID is for when something hurts.
Momentum beats a perfect plan.
